const EXPLAIN = {
A:[["Why semiology separates symptoms, signs and tests","A <b>simptom</b> is what the patient tells you, so you can only reach it through the <b>anamneză</b>. A <b>semn obiectiv</b> is what you find yourself on examination. Lab tests and imaging are <b>paraclinice</b>: they confirm or complete the clinical picture, they don't replace it. A <b>sindrom</b> is the step from a pile of findings to a pattern that points to a disease. The rest of the course is about building those patterns."],["Why the FOCG matters","The observation sheet is a legal, financial and scientific document as well as a medical one. That's why fixed points are recorded: allergies on page 1, the diagnosis at admission, at 72 hours and at discharge, and the <b>epicriza</b> as a summary. If something isn't written down, legally it didn't happen."]],
B:[["The anamnesis follows the order of diagnostic reasoning","Identity data (age, sex, occupation) already shift the odds: a miner's lungs, a farmer's zoonoses. The <b>7 caractere</b> of the main symptom work because each one separates diseases. Two chest pains with the same location can differ completely in <b>caracter</b>, <b>timing</b> and <b>factori de agravare</b>."],["How to remember the AHC categories","There are four ways a disease can \"run in a family\": shared genes plus environment (HTA, DZ, obesity), genes alone (hemophilia), shared microbes (TBC, hepatitis, lambliaza) and shared habits (diet). If you ask \"genes, germs or kitchen?\" you won't forget a category."],["Alcohol and tobacco as doses","One unit is about 10–13 g of pure ethanol. Example: 140 ml × 12% × 0.8 g/ml ≈ 13 g. So the >100 g/day threshold for a heavy drinker is roughly 8 units. <b>Pachete-ani</b> measures cumulative exposure, because the risk of BPCO and lung cancer grows with the total dose, not the daily dose."],["Medication side effects follow from mechanism","AINS block prostaglandins. Prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa and help the kidney excrete sodium. Without them you get digestive bleeding, plus salt retention that worsens HTA and heart failure. Immunosuppressants lower the immune surveillance that keeps both infections and tumours in check."]],
C:[["Weight change: is it calories, or water?","Gaining weight means either fat (energy in > energy out) or water (<b>retenție hidro-salină</b>). Losing several kilograms in a few days can only be water, for example a diuretic in a patient with edema. When the patient loses weight while eating more, calories are being wasted. In <b>DZ</b>, glucose is lost in the urine. In <b>hipertiroidism</b>, metabolism burns more."],["Oboseală vs astenie","Fatigue (<b>oboseală</b>) is a feeling of lost energy and has many causes. <b>Astenia</b> in this course means weakness you can measure. Measurable weakness sends you toward the nerves and muscles (miastenie, miopatie), not toward the long fatigue list."]],
D:[["Fever is a higher thermostat setting","In <b>febră</b>, pyrogens (cytokines) raise the set point in the anterior hypothalamus. At 37 °C the body now feels too cold, so it acts to warm up: vasoconstriction (<b>paloare</b>, cold skin) and shivering (<b>frison</b>, <b>rigor</b>). This is the <b>perioada incrementi</b>. Once the new set point is reached, heat production equals heat loss (<b>fastigiu</b>). When the set point drops again, the body is too hot: it vasodilates and sweats (<b>decrementi</b>). A sudden drop (<b>crisis</b>) can cause hypotension because of the vasodilation and fluid loss."],["Hypertermie: normal thermostat, failed cooling","In <b>hipertermie</b> the set point is normal, but the body can't lose enough heat (a hot environment, drugs that block sweating, malignant hyperthermia). That's why there are no circadian oscillations. It is also why antipyretics, which act on the hypothalamic set point, don't help."],["Fever curves reflect how the cause behaves","In malaria, the parasites mature inside red blood cells in synchronized cycles, so the attacks come at fixed intervals: <b>terță</b> (every 48 h) or <b>cuartă</b> (every 72 h). <b>Bifazică</b> means a phase of microbes in the blood, then a phase of localization in an organ. In <b>intermitentă</b>, the temperature returns to normal between spikes. That pattern fits pus collections that release bacteria periodically (abscesses, urinary or biliary infection). <b>Continuă</b> means sustained high fever with little daily variation (bacterial pneumonia, typhoid)."],["Why bradycardia with fever is a clue","The pulse normally rises by roughly 10 beats/min per °C. A slow pulse during fever is unexpected, so it narrows the list to typhoid, brucellosis, leptospirosis, drug fever and simulated fever."],["Thresholds to know cold","Normal is 36,8 ± 0,4 °C. Axillary fever is above 37,2 °C in the morning or 37,7 °C in the evening. Body temperature is lowest around 06:00 and highest around 18:00, which is why the morning threshold is lower. Rectal temperature reads 0,6 °C higher than axillary and sublingual 0,2 °C higher."]],
E:[["Forced positions make sense if you ask what they relieve","<b>Ortopnee</b>: sitting up lets blood pool in the legs, so less returns to the congested lungs. Gripping the edge of the bed fixes the shoulders, which lets the accessory breathing muscles work. <b>Pleurezie</b>: lying on the sick side leaves the healthy lung free to expand. <b>Peritonită</b>: the patient keeps still, because any movement or touch stretches the inflamed peritoneum. <b>Poziția pernei</b> in pericardial effusion and <b>squatting</b> in Fallot are both positions the patient finds because they make breathing easier."],["Meningeal signs","Inflamed meninges hurt when they are stretched. Flexing the neck (<b>Brudzinski</b>) or sitting the trunk up (<b>Kernig</b>) pulls on the meninges and the nerve roots. The knees bend reflexively to release that tension. <b>Cocoș de pușcă</b> is the position that keeps the meninges least stretched."],["Decorticare vs decerebrare","When the lesion is above the brainstem (<b>decorticare</b>), lower centres still drive the arms into flexion: arms bent onto the chest (\"to the cor\"). A <b>trunchi cerebral</b> lesion (<b>decerebrare</b>) removes that drive, and extensor tone takes over all four limbs. Decerebration means a deeper lesion and a worse prognosis."]],
F:[["Lipotimie and sincopă are degrees of the same mechanism","Both come from the brain getting too little blood. Moderate hypoperfusion gives an incomplete loss of consciousness with warning symptoms (<b>lipotimie</b>), so the patient sits down and sphincter control is kept. Severe hypoperfusion gives complete loss with no warning (<b>sincopă</b>): the patient falls, gets injured and may be incontinent. Lying flat restores venous return to the brain, which is why recovery comes quickly once the patient is down."],["Why a criză comițială is different","A seizure is an electrical problem, not a perfusion problem. So it happens in any position. It has an <b>aură</b>, convulsions, tongue biting and a long period of confusion afterwards (~20 min, <b>obnubilare</b>) with amnesia. Syncope wakes up within minutes. Epilepsy wakes up slowly."],["The graded scale of consciousness","Ask two questions: does the patient answer, and is the answer correct? Correct but slow is <b>torpoare</b>. Slow and wrong is <b>obnubilare</b> (= coma grade I). No answer is <b>stupoare</b>, or coma grade II–III depending on the response to pain."],["Illusion, hallucination, phobia","An <b>iluzie</b> needs a real object that is misperceived. A <b>halucinație</b> has no object at all. A <b>fobie</b> has a known danger, while <b>anxietate</b> is fear of something unknown. Those differences are what the exam asks."]],
G:[["Why waist beats BMI for risk","<b>IMC</b> (kg/m²) measures overall size. Abdominal (visceral) fat is metabolically active and drives insulin resistance and cardiovascular risk. That's why waist above 88/102 cm and waist/hip ratio above 1 define the high-risk <b>obezitate abdominală</b>. The <b>android</b> vs <b>ginoid</b> trapezoid tells you where the fat sits."],["Two kinds of short stature","Thyroid hormone is needed for brain development, so hypothyroid dwarfism comes with low intelligence. Growth hormone deficiency (<b>hipofizar</b>) doesn't affect the brain, so intelligence is normal. The same hormone in excess causes <b>gigantism</b> in childhood and <b>acromegalie</b> in adults, after the growth plates have closed."],["Ginecomastie","Breast tissue grows when estrogen outweighs androgen. A cirrhotic liver can't break estrogen down, and spironolactone blocks androgens. Both tip the balance."]],
H:[["Group the facies by mechanism instead of memorizing 20","<b>Hormones</b>: thyroid (hyper/hypo), STH (acromegal), cortisol (Cushingoid). <b>Blood colour</b>: anemic, pletoric, cyanotic, mitral. <b>Fluid</b>: nephrotic (soft, white, pitting edema) vs hypothyroid myxedema (infiltrated, non-pitting). <b>Severe illness</b>: hipocratic. <b>Connective tissue</b>: sclerodermie (icoană bizantină), lupus (vespertilio). <b>Neuromuscular</b>: Parkinson, miastenie, facial palsy. <b>Infection/neoplasia</b>: vultuos, leonin, congenital syphilis."],["Facial palsy: why the forehead decides","The part of the facial nerve nucleus that controls the forehead receives fibres from <b>both</b> brain hemispheres. The lower face receives fibres from the opposite hemisphere only. A <b>central</b> lesion (stroke) removes one hemisphere's input: the forehead still works thanks to the other side, and only the lower face droops. A <b>peripheral</b> lesion cuts the nerve itself, so the whole half of the face is paralysed, including <b>lagoftalmie</b>."]],
I:[["Cyanosis and oxygen","Cyanosis shows when there is too much deoxygenated haemoglobin in the capillaries. <b>Cianoza rece</b>: blood moves slowly through constricted vessels and tissues pull out more O₂ (heart failure). <b>Cianoza caldă</b> from lung failure improves with O₂, because the extra oxygen reaches the blood in the lungs. Cyanosis from a <b>șunt intracardiac</b> doesn't improve, because the shunted blood bypasses the lungs entirely."],["The three types of icter follow where bilirubin gets stuck","Hemolysis produces more bilirubin than the liver can conjugate, so <b>indirect</b> bilirubin rises (<b>flavin</b>, pale yellow). Damaged hepatocytes fail at both conjugation and excretion, so both types rise (<b>rubin</b>). In cholestasis, bilirubin is conjugated but can't leave, so <b>direct</b> bilirubin backs up (<b>verdin</b>, greenish). The sclera holds bilirubin. Carotene doesn't stain it, which is why <b>pseudo-icter</b> leaves the sclera white."],["Addison's bronze colour","Without cortisol there is no negative feedback, so the pituitary overproduces ACTH. ACTH comes from the same precursor as melanocyte-stimulating hormone. The result is pigmentation that also reaches the gums, palm creases and axillae, a useful way to tell it from a tan."],["Vitropresiune: dilated vessel or leaked blood","Pressing glass on the skin empties dilated vessels, so erythema fades. Blood that has already leaked into the dermis (<b>peteșie</b>, <b>purpură</b>) can't be pushed away. That's why the test tells inflammation from bleeding."],["Arterita Horton","The artery wall is inflamed and thickened, so the vessel feels hard and tender and the pulse disappears. Jaw pain while chewing is ischemia of the masseter, the same mechanism as claudication in the legs. Treat it as urgent: the same arteritis can threaten vision."]],
J:[["Ptoză plus pupil: localize the lesion","Two structures lift the eyelid. The <b>levator</b> is the main one, supplied by <b>NC III</b>. A small smooth muscle helps, supplied by the <b>simpatic</b>. The sympathetic also dilates the pupil, while NC III carries the fibres that constrict it. A sympathetic lesion (<b>Horner</b>) gives a mild ptosis with a small pupil (<b>mioză</b>). An NC III lesion gives a marked ptosis with a large pupil (<b>midriază</b>), because the pupil-constricting fibres are cut."],["Pupils and toxins","Parasympathetic drive constricts the pupil. Organophosphates block the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine, so acetylcholine builds up and the pupils become pinpoint. Morphine and pilocarpine also constrict. Atropine blocks those receptors, so the pupils dilate. Fixed bilateral midriază means the brainstem has stopped working (clinical death)."],["Coloured rings and sclerae","Copper deposits in the cornea give the brown-green <b>Kayser-Fleischer</b> ring (Wilson). Lipid gives the grey <b>gerontoxon</b> (atherosclerosis). A thin sclera lets the dark layer underneath show through as <b>blue</b> (fragilitas ossium). Silver gives <b>black</b> sclerae (argirie)."]],
K:[["Rinne and Weber in one idea","In <b>conductive</b> (transmisie) loss, air conduction is blocked but bone conduction bypasses the middle ear. So in Rinne, bone ≥ air. The blocked ear also hears less background noise, so in Weber the vibration sounds louder on the <b>affected</b> side (try it: plug one ear and hum). In <b>sensorineural</b> (percepție, NC VIII) loss, the cochlea or nerve is damaged. Both air and bone are reduced but air stays longer than bone, and Weber sounds louder on the <b>healthy</b> side."],["Nose findings to remember","A perforated septum or ulcerated mucosa suggests intranasal cocaine or amphetamines. A collapsed bridge (<b>nas în șa</b>) is the congenital syphilis sign, and it appears in the facies list too. Sinus tenderness is checked over the bone, at the inner third of the eyebrow (frontal) and below the inner third of the cheekbone (maxillary)."]],
L:[["Normal vs pathological lymph nodes","Normal superficial nodes are small (3–15 mm), elastic, mobile and painless. Tenderness plus inflammation means <b>adenită</b>, usually infection draining from nearby (<b>satelită</b>). Use the terms in the right order: <b>adenomegalie</b> only means enlarged, <b>adenopatie</b> means enlarged for a pathological reason, <b>adenită</b> means inflamed."],["Why the thyroid moves when you swallow","The thyroid is attached to the larynx, so it rises with the cricoid cartilage on swallowing. That is how you tell a thyroid mass from other neck lumps. Palpate the lobes from behind the patient. The isthmus lies over tracheal rings 2–4."],["Tracheal deviation","The trachea sits in the midline because the pressures on each side are balanced. A mediastinal mass, a large effusion or a tension pneumothorax pushes it to the opposite side."]],
M:[["Reading the hands","The hands carry many systemic signs. <b>Eritroză palmară</b> and Dupuytren point to the liver and alcohol. Warm vs cold palmar cyanosis tells lung from heart, the same logic as in the face. <b>Raynaud</b> is a spasm of the finger arteries: white (no flow), then blue (deoxygenated blood). Non-selective betablockers cause it because they block the receptors that relax the vessels, leaving constriction unopposed."],["Nails record what happened to the body","The nail grows slowly from its root, so a toxic or illness episode leaves a transverse line (<b>Mees</b>) that moves forward over time. You can roughly date the exposure from its distance to the root. Iron deficiency makes the nail thin and spoon-shaped (<b>koilonichie</b>). Small clots and immune deposits from infected heart valves show up as <b>hemoragii în așchie</b> and <b>noduli Osler</b>."],["Hipocratism digital","The common thread is chronic disease where blood reaches the tissues without passing properly through the lungs: lung suppuration and cancer, cyanogenic heart disease, endocarditis, inflammatory bowel disease, polycythemia. Growth factors build up and the tissue under the nail grows. When you see clubbing, go through the four groups: lung, heart, gut, blood."],["Lesion vocabulary is about depth and content","Flat lesions are colour changes: <b>maculă</b> under 1 cm, <b>pată</b> 1 cm or larger. Raised solid lesions grow papulă → nodul → tumoră. Fluid-filled lesions are veziculă/pustulă under 0,5 cm and bulă above. Loss of tissue: an <b>eroziune</b> stays in the epidermis and heals without a scar. An <b>ulcerație</b> reaches the dermis and leaves a scar. Bleeding into the skin is ordered by size and depth: peteșie → purpură → echimoză → hematom."]],
N:[["Upper vs lower motor neuron is the key distinction","A <b>pyramidal</b> lesion (upper motor neuron, as in stroke) removes the brain's braking of the spinal reflexes. After an initial <b>flasc</b> phase, tone and reflexes increase: <b>spastic</b>, ROT exagerate, Babinski pozitiv. A lesion of the reflex arc itself (nerve, spinal cord segment, muscle) interrupts the circuit, so reflexes drop or disappear. Nearly every reflex question is asking which of these two you are looking at."],["Dismetrie: cerebellum vs proprioception","To hit a target you need to know where your limb is (proprioception, carried in the posterior columns) and you need the cerebellum to coordinate the movement. In <b>tabetic</b> disease, proprioception is lost but vision can stand in for it, so the patient fails mainly with the eyes closed. In <b>cerebeloasă</b> disease, the coordination itself is broken and vision can't fix it. The same logic gives you Romberg and the two ataxic gaits."],["Artrită vs artroză","An <b>artrită</b> is inflammation of the joint lining. In rheumatoid arthritis this damages the knuckle joints and pulls the fingers toward the ulnar side (<b>labă de cârtiță</b>). An <b>artroză</b> is wear: bony nodules at the middle finger joints (Bouchard) and the end joints (Heberden). Memory trick: Heberden = the finger <b>H</b>ead (tip). <b>Redoare</b> is reversible. <b>Anchiloză</b> is permanent."]],
O:[["Classify tremor by when it appears","<b>Repaus</b> (at rest, disappears on movement) means the basal ganglia, Parkinson. <b>Intențional</b> (on movement, worse near the target) means the cerebellum. <b>Postural</b> fine tremor with the arms held out comes from extra adrenergic drive (anxiety, hyperthyroidism). <b>Flapping</b> is a different thing: brief lapses of muscle tone while holding the wrists extended, because the brain is poisoned metabolically (liver, kidney or CO₂ failure)."],["Basal ganglia movements","Coreea, atetoză and distonie lie on a spectrum: quick and dancing, then slower and twisting, then sustained postures involving the trunk. All three come from the basal ganglia. Fenotiazine appear again here (distonie) and earlier for oro-facial dyskinesia."]],
P:[["Edema in one equation","Fluid leaves the capillary when the pressure inside rises (<b>IC dreaptă</b>, standing), when plasma proteins fall (<b>ciroză</b>, <b>nefrotic</b>, malnutrition), when the capillary wall leaks (inflammation, nifedipine), or when lymph can't drain. A systemic cause affects both legs, so the edema is <b>simetric</b>. A local cause (one vein, one lymph pathway, one infection) is <b>asimetric</b>. Lymphedema becomes rich in protein and fibrous, so it stops pitting."],["Painful or not","Pain points to inflammation or acute venous thrombosis. Those are the asymmetric edemas you must not miss (TVP can lead to pulmonary embolism). Lipedem only looks like edema: it is fat, hard and tender, and it spares the top of the foot."],["Babinski","Normally, stroking the outer sole curls the big toe downward. When the pyramidal tract is damaged, an older reflex pattern returns and the toe goes up. On one side, it means a unilateral pyramidal lesion. On both sides, it means a diffuse encephalopathy."],["Breast exam is systematic","Inspect in three arm positions, because retractions show when the pectoral muscles tense. Palpate lying down with the arm behind the head, so the tissue spreads over the chest wall. Cover all four quadrants and finish at the areola. Describe every nodule by size, tenderness and quadrant."]],
Q:[["Read the pulses from top to bottom","Femoral (below the inguinal ligament), popliteal (knee flexed 90°), tibial posterior (behind the medial malleolus), pedios (first interosseous space). The first pulse you can't feel tells you roughly where the artery is blocked. A bruit means the narrowing is partial and the flow is turbulent."],["Lasègue","Raising the straight leg stretches the sciatic nerve roots. If a herniated disc is pressing on a root, the stretch reproduces the pain. Record the angle at which pain starts. Adding adduction (<b>Bonnet</b>) stretches the roots further."],["Knee signs","<b>Clapa de pian</b>: you push the joint fluid under the kneecap, so it floats and bounces when tapped (effusion). <b>Sertar</b>: abnormal forward or sideways glide of the shin means a ligament is torn."]],
R:[["Balance needs 2 out of 3","Standing still uses vision, proprioception and the vestibular system. Closing the eyes in <b>Romberg</b> removes vision. If proprioception is lost (<b>tabetic</b>), the patient falls immediately, in any direction. A <b>vestibular</b> lesion pulls slowly toward the affected side, and closing the eyes changes little."],["Gait localizes the lesion","<b>Cosit</b>: one stiff, extended leg swung outward, with the arm flexed (stroke, hemiparesis). <b>Forfecat</b>: both legs spastic and crossing (paraparesis). <b>Stepat</b>: SPE supplies the muscles that lift the foot, so the foot hangs and the patient raises the knee high. <b>Talonat</b>: SPI supplies the calf muscles that push off and lift you onto your toes, so the patient walks on the heel. Physiologically SPI weakness is a loss of plantar flexion; the course text phrases it differently, so learn the course wording for the exam. <b>Trendelenburg</b>/<b>miopatic</b>: weak hip muscles can't stabilize the pelvis, so the patient waddles. <b>Parkinson</b>: small, rigid steps."],["Spine: when it started and what shape it has","A curve that is <b>unghiulară</b> (ghibus) means one vertebra has collapsed, for example from TBC (Pott). A <b>rotundă</b> curve in a young patient suggests Scheuermann or spondylitis, and in an older one osteoporosis. A spine that has lost its lumbar curve (<b>rectitudine</b>) and sacroiliac joints that hurt on palpation suggest early ankylosing spondylitis. Scoliosis is either muscle spasm (antalgic), weak muscles (paralytic) or compensation for a short leg."]]
};

